VI  - 25
IP  - 4
DP  - 2019 Jul
TI  - Outpatient talc administration via indwelling pleural catheters for malignant 
      effusions.
PG  - 380-383
LID - 10.1097/MCP.0000000000000587 [doi]
AB  - PURPOSE OF REVIEW: Malignant pleural effusion is a common cause of breathlessness 
      and signifies advanced disease. Common options for definitive pleural 
      intervention include insertion of an indwelling pleural catheter (IPC) or talc 
      pleurodesis. RECENT FINDINGS: Administration of graded talc through an IPC offers 
      an increased chance of pleurodesis compared with IPC drainage alone and is not 
      associated with a significant risk of adverse events. SUMMARY: In patients where 
      an ambulatory treatment pathway is preferred, the increased chance of pleurodesis 
      with talc administration via IPC can result in a faster time to device removal 
      and may be associated with better quality of life and symptom scores.
