TI  - ERS/EACTS statement on the management of malignant pleural effusions.
LID - 1800349 [pii]
LID - 10.1183/13993003.00349-2018 [doi]
AB  - Malignant pleural effusions (MPE) are a common pathology, treated by respiratory 
      physicians and thoracic surgeons alike. In recent years, several well-designed 
      randomised clinical trials have been published that have changed the landscape of 
      MPE management. The European Respiratory Society (ERS) and the European 
      Association for Cardio-Thoracic Surgery (EACTS) established a multidisciplinary 
      collaboration of clinicians with expertise in the management of MPE with the aim 
      of producing a comprehensive review of the scientific literature.Six areas of 
      interest were identified, including the optimum management of symptomatic MPE, 
      management of trapped lung in MPE, management of loculated MPE, prognostic 
      factors in MPE, whether there is a role for oncological therapies prior to 
      intervention for MPE and whether a histological diagnosis is always required in 
      MPE.The literature revealed that talc pleurodesis and indwelling pleural 
      catheters effectively manage the symptoms of MPE. There was limited evidence 
      regarding the management of trapped lung or loculated MPE. The LENT score was 
      identified as a validated tool for predicting survival in MPE, with Brims' 
      prognostic score demonstrating utility in mesothelioma prognostication. There was 
      no evidence to support the use of oncological therapies as an alternative to MPE 
      drainage, and the literature supported the use of tissue biopsy as the gold 
      standard for diagnosis and treatment planning.
