IS  - 2213-0071 (Electronic)
IS  - 2213-0071 (Linking)
VI  - 20
DP  - 2017
TI  - Transudative pleural effusion of malignant etiology: Rare but real.
PG  - 188-191
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2017.02.015 [doi]
AB  - A 62-year-old female presented to the emergency room with one-month history of 
      epigastric abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting. She endorsed progressive dyspnea 
      over two weeks. CT of the abdomen demonstrated bilateral pleural effusions and 
      pancreatic inflammation, so the working diagnosis was pancreatitis. A diagnostic 
      thoracentesis was performed and the pleural fluid analysis was classified as 
      transudate by Light's criteria. Given the atypical features in history and 
      concern for malignancy, fluid was sent for cytological examination and 
      immunohistochemistry which suggested a mucinous malignancy. EGD revealed poorly 
      differentiated signet ring cell adenocarcinoma of stomach. Patient underwent 
      placement of indwelling pleural catheters for symptomatic improvement and was 
      discharged to hospice. The decision whether to routinely send transudative 
      effusions for cytological evaluation remains controversial. This case 
      demonstrates the importance of using clinical judgement to guide that decision.
